- **1–3%** reply rate on managed cold email, with 20%+ of replies positive (Source: [Myntmore Benchmark Report](/resources/blogs/b2b-outbound-benchmark-report-2026))
- **8 touches** average number needed before a prospect engages (Source: RAIN Group)
- **45 days** for a new domain to reach full send volume; campaigns start at low volume much sooner (Source: Instantly.ai Data)

## Why a single email isn't a strategy

Most prospects don't reply to the first email, not because the offer is bad, but because timing, attention, and inbox noise are all working against a single message. A sequence spreads the same core idea across several touches, each with a different job, so a prospect who wasn't ready on day 1 still has four more chances to engage when the timing is better.

## The 5-touch sequence, email by email

Every touch below replies in the same email thread, not a new one, which keeps context visible and avoids re-triggering spam filters with a fresh subject line each time.

Day 1

### The opener

Goal: Earn a reply, not a sale.

> **Subject:** quick one on \[specific trigger, e.g. your Q3 hiring push\]
>
> Hi \[First Name\],
>
> Noticed \[specific, real trigger: a job posting, a funding announcement, a product launch, a LinkedIn post\]. Companies in a similar spot usually run into \[one specific, recognizable problem\] around that stage.
>
> We've helped teams like \[type of company\] fix exactly that. Worth a quick look at how?
>
> \[Your name\]

Day 3

### The value-add

Goal: Give something useful before asking for anything.

> **Subject:** re: quick one on \[trigger\] (same thread)
>
> Hi \[First Name\],
>
> Following up with something that might be useful regardless of whether we ever talk: \[one specific insight, stat, or short framework relevant to their role\].
>
> If it's relevant, happy to share the fuller version.
>
> \[Your name\]

Day 7

### The reframe

Goal: Approach the same problem from a different angle in case the first framing didn't land.

> **Subject:** re: quick one on \[trigger\] (same thread)
>
> Hi \[First Name\],
>
> Different angle on this: most \[role, e.g. VP Sales\] we talk to aren't short on leads, they're short on \[more specific bottleneck, e.g. qualified meetings on their calendar\]. Is that closer to what you're seeing, or is it a different piece of the puzzle for you?
>
> \[Your name\]

Day 12

### The proof point

Goal: Show it's worked for someone comparable, without overclaiming.

> **Subject:** re: quick one on \[trigger\] (same thread)
>
> Hi \[First Name\],
>
> Thought this might resonate: \[one-line summary of a relevant, real result or case study\], similar situation to what I mentioned earlier.
>
> Here's the full breakdown if useful: \[link to a real case study\].
>
> Open to a short call to see if the same approach fits your setup?
>
> \[Your name\]

Day 18

### The breakup

Goal: Close the loop honestly, which often gets more replies than the four emails before it.

> **Subject:** re: quick one on \[trigger\] (same thread)
>
> Hi \[First Name\],
>
> Haven't heard back, so I'll assume the timing isn't right or this isn't a priority right now. No hard feelings, I'll stop following up here.
>
> If that changes, my door's open, just reply whenever.
>
> \[Your name\]

## A filled-in example sequence

Here is the same template written out for one scenario: a sales-tools company emailing a VP of Sales whose team is hiring SDRs. Names and details are illustrative; swap in a real trigger for every prospect.

> Day 1
>
> **Subject:** quick one on your sales hiring
>
> Hi Priya,
>
> Saw you're hiring three SDRs this quarter. Teams adding SDRs that fast usually find ramp time, not headcount, is what slows pipeline down.
>
> We help B2B SaaS teams get meetings flowing while new hires ramp. Worth a quick look at how?
>
> Arjun

> Day 3
>
> **Subject:** re: quick one on your sales hiring
>
> Hi Priya,
>
> Useful either way: the teams we see ramp SDRs fastest give them a pre-built list of in-market accounts in week one, instead of asking them to prospect from scratch.
>
> Happy to share the checklist we use.
>
> Arjun

> Day 7
>
> **Subject:** re: quick one on your sales hiring
>
> Hi Priya,
>
> Different angle: most VPs of Sales we talk to aren't short on leads, they're short on qualified meetings on the calendar. Is that closer to what you're seeing?
>
> Arjun

> Day 12
>
> **Subject:** re: quick one on your sales hiring
>
> Hi Priya,
>
> A Series A SaaS team in a similar spot went from zero to 40 qualified meetings in 8 weeks. Here's how: \[case study link\]
>
> Open to 15 minutes to see if it fits your setup?
>
> Arjun

> Day 18
>
> **Subject:** re: quick one on your sales hiring
>
> Hi Priya,
>
> I'll assume the timing isn't right, so I'll stop here. If pipeline becomes the priority, just reply and I'll pick it up.
>
> Arjun

## What makes a subject line work here

Lowercase, specific, and undramatic beats clever every time. "quick one on \[trigger\]" outperforms a punchy hook because it reads like a message from a person, not a campaign. Once the thread is established, every follow-up keeps the same subject line with "re:" in front, which is exactly what a real ongoing conversation looks like in an inbox.

## Knowing when to stop

Five touches over about three weeks is enough to know. Beyond that, additional emails to a non-responder mostly generate spam complaints and unsubscribes rather than replies. If someone hasn't engaged by the breakup email, the honest move is to close the loop, remove them from the active sequence, and revisit in a future quarter rather than continuing to push.

## Pairing this with LinkedIn

The same five-touch logic runs in parallel on LinkedIn, and running both channels together consistently outperforms either alone, since a prospect who ignores an email might still accept a connection request, or vice versa. For the LinkedIn-specific version of this sequence, see [LinkedIn Outreach Sequences That Actually Get Replies](/resources/blogs/linkedin-outreach-sequences). And before sending any of this at volume, make sure the sending domain is actually warmed up, covered in our [cold email deliverability guide](/resources/blogs/cold-email-deliverability-guide). For the exact tools we use to scrape, enrich, verify, and send, plus a second sequence with live variants, see [the cold email stack behind an extra $7,000 in revenue](/resources/guides/cold-email-tech-stack). And once the replies start landing, here is [what to do in the first hour after a prospect responds](/resources/blogs/handling-cold-email-replies).
